<DocsLayout title="Agent accounts">
  <h1>Agent accounts</h1>
  <p>
    An <strong>agent account</strong> is a non-human account that a person
    owns: an assistant, a bot, or an automation that acts in your applications
    under its own user id. Applications can show it, mention it and attribute
    work to it like any other account. Identity records two things about it:
    that it is an agent, and who owns it. What an agent may do in your
    applications is decided there, not here.
  </p>

  <h2>Turning it on</h2>
  <p>
    Agent accounts are off until the deployment sets
    <code>GATEWAY_AGENTS_ENABLED=true</code>. While off, every RPC on this page
    answers <code>FAILED_PRECONDITION</code>. The sign-in refusals below hold
    either way.
  </p>

  <h2>What an agent is</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      A user row with <code>kind</code> set to <code>USER_KIND_AGENT</code> and
      <code>owner_user_id</code> naming its owner, an active person in the same
      project. Every other account reports <code>USER_KIND_PERSON</code> and no
      owner.
    </li>
    <li>
      It has a <strong>name</strong> and an optional <strong>avatar URL</strong>
      (an absolute <code>https</code> URL). It has no email, no
      username and no account address, and its role is always
      <code>member</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      It has <strong>no way to sign in</strong>. Password, email code, magic
      link, provider sign-in (web, native and hosted), passkey, two-step, QR
      approval and invitations all refuse an agent with
      <code>PERMISSION_DENIED</code>, and identity records an
      <code>agent_sign_in_refused</code> audit entry. A credential cannot be
      attached to one either: passkeys, linked providers, phone numbers,
      two-step, identity verification and an admin password reset answer
      <code>FAILED_PRECONDITION</code>. How an agent obtains tokens is not part
      of this release.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Managing agents</h2>
  <p>
    The owner manages their agents from their own session. A project
    <strong>admin</strong> can manage anyone's, with the same RPCs.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <code>CreateAgent</code>: name and avatar. The caller owns the new agent;
      an admin may set <code>owner_user_id</code> to create one for another
      person. A person owns at most
      <code>GATEWAY_AGENTS_MAX_PER_OWNER</code> agents (default 25, at most
      500); past that
      the call answers <code>RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED</code>. Creation is
      rate-limited per IP at the sign-up rate.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>ListAgents</code>: the caller's agents, or, for an admin, those of
      the account named in <code>owner_user_id</code>, including one that has
      been deleted. The list is complete: a merge can leave an owner with more
      agents than the limit.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>UpdateAgent</code>: replaces the name and avatar. The admin
      <code>UpdateUser</code> refuses an agent.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>TransferAgent</code>: gives the agent to another active person in
      the project, within that person's limit, and ends the agent's sessions.
      The previous owner loses it at once.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>DeactivateAgent</code> and <code>ReactivateAgent</code>: suspend
      and restore it. Deactivating ends its sessions. Both are idempotent.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>DeleteAgent</code>: erases it the way <code>DeleteUser</code>
      erases an account.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Anyone else gets the same <code>PERMISSION_DENIED</code> whether the id
    names an agent, a person or nothing, so the RPCs cannot be used to find
    out which ids are agents. An agent cannot manage agents, and neither can
    an anonymous account.
  </p>

  <h2>An agent stands with its owner</h2>
  <p>
    An agent is usable only while it is active <strong>and</strong> its owner
    is an active person. This is checked every time a token is issued for the
    agent, refresh included, so it holds even for an owner who is suspended
    some other way. When the owner is deactivated, scheduled for deletion,
    deleted or deprovisioned by SCIM, their agents' sessions are
    revoked along with their own. Reactivating the owner makes the agents
    usable again; nothing was written to them.
  </p>
  <p>
    Deleting an owner does <strong>not</strong> delete their agents. They keep
    naming the deleted owner and stay unusable until an admin transfers or
    deletes them; <code>ListAgents</code> with the deleted owner's id finds
    them. When an owner is merged into another account, their agents move to
    the survivor. An agent itself is never merged.
  </p>

  <h2>Tokens</h2>
  <p>
    An agent's access token carries <code>"kind": "agent"</code>. A person's
    token carries no <code>kind</code> claim, so it is unchanged. An
    application that must never treat an agent as a person checks this claim,
    or the <code>kind</code> on the <code>User</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Where agents appear</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      The admin <code>ListUsers</code> leaves agents out unless
      <code>include_agents</code> is set; <code>GetUser</code> returns one by
      id.
    </li>
    <li>
      The <a href="/identity/docs/users/directory-lookup">directory lookup</a> never returns
      them: it resolves email addresses, and an agent has none.
    </li>
    <li>SCIM does not see them.</li>
    <li>
      Webhook payloads for an agent carry <code>kind</code> and
      <code>owner_user_id</code>. A person's payload is unchanged.
      <code>CreateAgent</code> sends <code>user.created</code>; transferring,
      renaming or reactivating sends <code>user.updated</code>; deactivating
      sends <code>user.deactivated</code>; deleting sends the same events as
      deleting any account.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Audit</h2>
  <p>
    Every change is recorded: <code>agent_created</code>,
    <code>agent_updated</code>, <code>agent_transferred</code>,
    <code>agent_deactivated</code>, <code>agent_reactivated</code> and
    <code>agent_deleted</code>, with the caller as actor and the agent as
    target, and refusals as failures. <code>agents_listed</code> records an
    admin listing another person's agents (the owner as target) and a
    refused listing; listing your own is not recorded. A refused sign-in is
    <code>agent_sign_in_refused</code>.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
